# Line endings are a repo policy here, not an artifact of each developer's
# git configuration.
#
# Every NeoOS repo stores LF in its index and runs CI on ubuntu, but a Windows
# clone with core.autocrlf=true materializes text files as CRLF. Several gates in
# this estate digest or string-match file bytes:
#
#   - the xunit source-shape assertions over contract sources,
#   - the cross-repo "vendored file" checksums and the MiniApp.DevPack
#     directory fingerprint,
#   - the generated ABI / factory-artifact snapshots compared with --check,
#   - the Nitro worker engine-wrapper hash whitelist,
#   - prettier, whose endOfLine default this repo's own .prettierrc sets to "lf".
#
# All of them reported false failures on a Windows checkout while passing CI,
# which trains developers to distrust exactly the gates meant to catch real
# drift. Pinning eol=lf makes the checkout match the index so those checks
# measure content on every platform.
#
# Adding this file does not rewrite an existing working tree. To convert one in
# place, do it on a clean tree so the renormalization lands as its own commit:
#
#   git add --renormalize .
#   git commit -m "chore: normalize line endings to LF"

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch scripts must keep CRLF or they can stop parsing.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Byte-exact payloads: never line-ending-converted, because something digests
# them as-is (integrity pins, image assets, compiled Neo executables).
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.svg text eol=lf
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.bmp binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf binary
*.mp3 binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary
*.zip binary
*.tgz binary
*.gz binary
*.bz2 binary
*.zst binary
*.nef binary
*.dll binary
*.pdb binary
*.exe binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
